<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-dev-containers"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="The container build, MCP transport, key rotation, container recreation, and an audited action were live-tested with Dev Containers CLI 0.89.0 on September 7, 2026; VS Code and a signed-in agent were not part of that test."
      toc={[
        {"start", "Start the connection"},
        {"image", "Add the bridge to the image"},
        {"container", "Restrict the container"},
        {"configure", "Configure the agent"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"boundary", "Know the boundary"},
        {"rebuild", "Rebuild and rotate"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-dev-containers">
        <:dek>
          Put a CLI agent and the emisar bridge inside a development container with a small host
          filesystem view and no extra Linux capabilities.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A Dev Container is useful when your project already has a containerized toolchain. This
        guide runs the CLI agent from the container terminal. It does not treat the VS Code
        extension, forwarded credentials, or a mounted host Docker socket as isolated by default.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Docker and either the VS Code Dev Containers extension or the standalone Dev Containers
          CLI. Start from a working
          <.docs_inline_code>.devcontainer</.docs_inline_code>
          setup that already includes your CLI agent and its runtime.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          A non-root container user. The example below uses the
          <.docs_inline_code>node</.docs_inline_code>
          user from <.docs_inline_code>node:22-bookworm</.docs_inline_code>.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          Complete the
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          first if you do not have a runner yet.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="start">1. Start the connection</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect an agent</a>, choose the CLI agent you will run, select <strong class="text-zinc-200">Linux</strong>, and expand <strong class="text-zinc-200">Set up manually</strong>. Keep the page open so you can copy
        its reveal-once key into the container after the image is built.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="image">2. Add the bridge to the image</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Add the GitHub CLI and emisar bridge to your existing Dev Container Dockerfile. The GitHub
        CLI lets the installer verify the bridge's release attestation. This complete example uses
        the same Debian-based image as the qualification; merge the two
        <.docs_inline_code>RUN</.docs_inline_code>
        steps into your image if it already has a base and agent toolchain.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label=".devcontainer/Dockerfile">FROM node:22-bookworm

USER root
RUN install -d -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings \
 &amp;&amp; curl -fsSL https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
      -o /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
 &amp;&amp; chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
 &amp;&amp; printf '%s\n' "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main" \
      &gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list \
 &amp;&amp; apt-get update \
 &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gh
RUN curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh -o /tmp/install-mcp.sh \
 &amp;&amp; bash /tmp/install-mcp.sh --yes --install-dir /usr/local/bin \
 &amp;&amp; install -d -m 700 -o node -g node /config
USER node</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="container">3. Restrict the container</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Add the bridge-state volume and process restrictions to <.docs_inline_code>.devcontainer/devcontainer.json</.docs_inline_code>.
        Keep the rest of your existing build and editor settings.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label=".devcontainer/devcontainer.json">{
  "name": "emisar agent",
  "build": { "dockerfile": "Dockerfile" },
  "containerUser": "node",
  "remoteUser": "node",
  "updateRemoteUserUID": false,
  "userEnvProbe": "none",
  "mounts": [
    "source=emisar-devcontainer-config,target=/config,type=volume"
  ],
  "runArgs": [
    "--cap-drop=ALL",
    "--security-opt=no-new-privileges"
  ]
}</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Rebuild and reopen the container. In VS Code, use <strong class="text-zinc-200">Dev Containers: Rebuild Container</strong>. With the CLI,
        run <.docs_inline_code>devcontainer up --workspace-folder .</.docs_inline_code>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="configure">4. Configure the agent</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In the container, save the configuration from step 1 at the Linux path shown by the
        console. Change its bridge command to
        <.docs_inline_code>/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
        if needed, and add
        <.docs_inline_code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/config</.docs_inline_code>
        to the bridge environment. That volume retains the bridge's rotated successor when the
        container is replaced.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For Codex, save this as <.docs_inline_code>~/.codex/config.toml</.docs_inline_code>, replacing
        the key with the value shown in the console:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="~/.codex/config.toml">[mcp_servers.emisar]
command = "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp"
env = { EMISAR_URL = "https://emisar.dev", EMISAR_API_KEY = "emk-replace-with-your-key", EMISAR_CLIENT = "codex", XDG_CONFIG_HOME = "/config" }</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Merge this table with existing Codex settings rather than replacing the file. For another
        agent, use the config shape and path shown by Connect an agent, then add the same
        <.docs_inline_code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</.docs_inline_code>
        variable to its emisar server environment. Keep the key out of the Dockerfile, <.docs_inline_code>devcontainer.json</.docs_inline_code>, image layers, and repository.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">5. Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>Start a fresh agent process in the container and confirm that it lists emisar.</li>
        <li>
          Ask: <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Use emisar to find a runner with
          linux.uptime, run it, and show me the output.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          Complete any approval required by your <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >emisar policy</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          and confirm the action, runner, operator, and result.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="boundary">Know the boundary</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          A Dev Container can read every file mounted into it. Remove project secrets and avoid
          mounting your home directory, SSH agent, cloud configuration, or host Docker socket.
        </li>
        <li>
          The configuration above drops Linux capabilities and blocks privilege escalation. It
          does not restrict outbound networking, and it does not hide secrets already present in
          the project workspace.
        </li>
        <li>
          VS Code can share Git credentials or forward an SSH agent. Review its
          <.link
            href="https://code.visualstudio.com/remote/advancedcontainers/sharing-git-credentials"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >credential-sharing settings</.link>
          separately before treating an editor session as isolated.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="rebuild">Rebuild and rotate</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar-devcontainer-config</.docs_inline_code>
        volume when rebuilding. The tested bridge retained its rotated successor there and accepted
        the original bootstrap configuration after the container was recreated. The agent's own
        config is not in that volume: preserve it in a separate private volume or provision it
        again after a rebuild. If the state volume is lost, create a fresh key from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        and replace the old config.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
